<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>

    <!-- THE PASSWORD-RESET EMAIL: whose name is on it, and who signs it.

         This is the one email a locked-out user is guaranteed to read
         carefully, and it was the last customer-facing mail still carrying
         Odoo's name. `sapian_theme_mail` fixed the NOTIFICATION LAYOUTS
         (mail.mail_notification_layout and mail.mail_notification_light);
         this template is not a notification and uses neither — it is a
         standalone ir.ui.view rendered directly by
         `res.users.action_reset_password` (auth_signup/models/res_users.py:206
         in Odoo 19), so nothing in that PR could reach it.

         MEASURED ON A DATABASE WITH sapian_theme_mail INSTALLED, rendering the
         template through the product's own call, as the PUBLIC user (which is
         who renders it when somebody clicks "Reset password" on the login
         page):

             Powered by <a href="https://www.odoo.com?utm_source=db&amp;utm_medium=auth"
                           style="color: #14454F;">Odoo</a>

             Thanks,<br/><div>Public user</div>

         Read the colour on that link. It is OUR teal, because
         `sapian_theme_mail` seeded `email_secondary_color` and this template
         paints the attribution with it. The house brand was being used to
         advertise a competitor, in the client's own mail, over the client's
         own name.

         ── 1. THE ATTRIBUTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────

         Same decision, same words, same switch as
         sapian_theme_mail/views/mail_attribution.xml — deliberately, because a
         client who unticks one box expects it to mean the same thing
         everywhere. "Powered by Odoo" is simply wrong and goes
         unconditionally; "Powered by SapianERP" replaces it, on by default,
         and `res.company.sapian_email_attribution` switches it off.

         OFF MEANS OFF. The whole grey band goes with it — not just the link,
         and never a fallback to Odoo. Replacing only the anchor would leave a
         dangling "Powered by" over an empty strip, which is the failure mode
         the mail-layout override was written to avoid, so this one replaces
         the entire <tr>.

         THE ANCHOR IS THE ODOO LINK'S GRANDPARENT CELL, matched through it:
         `//td[table//a[contains(@href, 'odoo.com')]]/..`. Matched by HREF
         rather than by position for the same reason as the login page and the
         mail layouts — the surrounding table is a stack of visually identical
         `<td align="center" style="min-width: 590px;">` cells, and the only
         thing that distinguishes this one is what is inside it. Nothing else
         in this template points at odoo.com.

         ── 2. THE SIGNATURE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

         Upstream signs the mail with `user.signature`, where `user` is
         `self.env.user` at render time (mail_render_mixin.py:329). On the path
         that matters that is the PUBLIC user, because the reset form is served
         to somebody who is by definition not logged in: the controller calls
         `request.env['res.users'].sudo()`, and sudo changes the privilege, not
         the identity. So the mail arrives signed "Public user".

         IT NOW SIGNS AS THE COMPANY, always — not "the company when there is
         no real user". Three reasons, and the first is the one that decides it:

         * The message is not from a person. Nobody at the client composed it;
           a machine sent it because somebody forgot a password. A signature
           names a sender, and the honest sender here is the company.

         * The alternative — keep `user.signature` when an internal user
           happens to trigger it — makes the SAME email say two different
           things depending on who clicked. An admin using Users >  "Send
           password reset instructions" would put their personal name and
           signature block in front of a portal customer who has no
           relationship with them and did not ask for it. That is a small
           information disclosure and a large inconsistency, and it is
           untestable: the content would depend on the session.

         * Under the ownership rule this repo works to, outward-facing
           correspondence carries the CLIENT's identity, and the client's
           identity is their company. The footer of this same mail already
           carries the company name, phone, email and website — signing with
           it is agreement, not repetition of something arbitrary.

         `object.company_id` rather than `user.company_id`: `object` is the user
         being reset, so the mail is signed by THEIR company. On a
         multi-company tenant that is the only correct choice — the public user
         has no meaningful company at all. -->

    <template id="reset_password_email_sapian"
              inherit_id="auth_signup.reset_password_email"
              name="SapianERP: password reset mail attribution and signature">

        <!-- The signature. `position="replace"` on the whole conditional, so
             the "Thanks," text node that precedes it is kept and only what
             follows it changes. -->
        <xpath expr="//t[@t-if='user.signature']" position="replace">
            <br/>
            <t t-out="object.company_id.name or ''">YourCompany</t>
        </xpath>

        <!-- The attribution band. -->
        <xpath expr="//td[table//a[contains(@href, 'odoo.com')]]/.." position="replace">
            <tr t-if="object.company_id.sapian_email_attribution">
                <td align="center" style="min-width: 590px;">
                    <table border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="590"
                           style="min-width: 590px; background-color: #F1F1F1; color: #454748; padding: 8px; border-collapse:separate;">
                        <tr><td style="text-align: center; font-size: 13px;">
                            Powered by <a target="_blank" href="https://sapiantech.com"
                                          t-attf-style="color: {{object.company_id.email_secondary_color or '#14454F'}};">SapianERP</a>
                        </td></tr>
                    </table>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </xpath>
    </template>

</odoo>
